<p>Today I tried to add a couple of recently purchased books to my library database using <a href="https://leancrew.com/all-this/2025/04/adding-books-to-my-library-database/">my <code>lccn2library</code> script</a>. It failed because the Library of Congress has either moved or deleted the files <code>lccn2library</code> relies on.</p>
<p>Let’s say I was trying to add <a href="https://lccn.loc.gov/26010978"><em>Applied Elasticity</em></a> by Timoshenko and Lessells.<sup id="fnref:westinghouse"><a href="#fn:westinghouse" rel="footnote">1</a></sup> If you go to the LoC’s MARC record for the book and click on the Download link, you’ll see a popup that looks like this:</p>
<p><img alt="LoC downloads popup" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2026/20261008-LoC%20downloads%20popup.png" title="LoC downloads popup" width="60%"/></p>
<p>So it looks like you can click the MODS link to get the <a href="https://www.loc.gov/standards/mods/">MODS file</a> for the book. That’s the file my script parses to get the information to add to the database. The MODS link for this book is</p>
<pre><code>https://lccn.loc.gov/26010978/mods
</code></pre>
<p>which is the URL form that <code>lccn2library</code> expects. Unfortunately, that link returns an empty file. And not just for that book, but for every book I tried. What does the little northeast-pointing arrow mean? Beats me. You’d think it was an indication that the MODS link was pointing to some external site, but that’s not the case.</p>
<p>Maybe I’ve caught the LoC in the middle of a transition to a new system for storing records in various formats. Maybe I’ll return in a month or two to find that everything is working again—perhaps with new URLs for the different formats. I hope so.</p>
<p>But until that hoped-for day, I’m pissed at the LoC because I have to add books to my database by hand using the <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/"><code>sqlite3</code></a> interactive command-line tool. Which is slow but not terrible, especially since I’m adding only a book or two at a time.</p>
<p>The command to add a new book to the database is</p>
<pre><code>INSERT INTO book(title, subtitle, volume, edition, publisher, published, lccn, loc, added)
VALUES(&lt;list of values separated by commas&gt;);
</code></pre>
<p>The book will be assigned an ID automatically. To get it, I run</p>
<pre><code>SELECT id,title FROM book WHERE added IS "2026-10-08";
</code></pre>
<p>The first field in the output will be the ID. The date must be a string with the current date in yyyy-mm-dd format.</p>
<p>The new book may be written by an author already in the database. To check if that’s the case, I run</p>
<pre><code>SELECT * FROM author WHERE name LIKE "%&lt;portion of name&gt;%";
</code></pre>
<p>If the author’s already in the database, that command will return their ID. If not, it returns nothing. I then run</p>
<pre><code>INSERT INTO author(name) VALUES("&lt;full name&gt;");
</code></pre>
<p>and then the <code>SELECT</code> command above to get the author’s ID.</p>
<p>The final step is to add a record to the <code>book_author</code> table to tie the book and author together. That’s</p>
<pre><code>INSERT INTO book_author(book_id, author_id) VALUES(&lt;book ID&gt;, &lt;author ID&gt;);
</code></pre>
<p>As I said, this is slow because it’s several steps, but none of the steps are difficult.</p>
<p>What will I do if the LoC never fixes the problem of the missing MODS files? I suppose I could parse the <a href="https://www.loc.gov/marc/umb/um01to06.html">MARC records</a>, which are still accessible, but that would be a pain in the ass. Maybe I’ll build a little interactive script where I can just type in the necessary information and have the script generate and run the SQL commands. I hope it doesn’t come to that.</p>
